1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 Be7 6. Qe2 Nd6 7. Bxc6 bxc6 8.
dxe5 Nb7 9. Nc3 Nc5 10. Nd4 O-O ({If} 10... Ba6 11. Qg4) 11. Rd1 {So far book.
White now threatens Nxc6.} Qe8 12. Be3 d5 13. Qf3 ({Somewhat risky.} 13. Nxc6
Qxc6 14. Nxd5 Bd8 {, and White has hardly sufficient value for his piece. This
thought apparently influences the next move on both sides, and the ultimate
result is the loss of a Pawn.}) 13... Bd7 ({Which meets immediate requirements,
but gives White time to institute a double attack with his queen on c-pawn and
g-pawn, and thus reflects on Black's 12th move.} 13... f6 {might be played.})
14. Qg3 f6 {This comes in now as a deep move, more attractive on this account
than the regulation reply 14...Kh8.} 15. Bf4 ({Strengthening the attack while
clearing the way for Re1. Black’s reply (Rc8) is still good enough to stop
exf6 and Qxc7. Suppose} 15. exf6 Rxf6 16. Qxc7 Bd6 17. Qa5 Qh5 {, etc}) (15.
Bh6 {, although not quite so disastrous, offers no inducement.}) 15... Rc8 16.
Re1 fxe5 {No doubt the simplest of the various alternatives at his disposal -
Qf7, Qg6, and Ne6 - which require careful and exhaustive analysis. It looks,
however, as if he had missed the continuation adopted by White.} 17. Bxe5 Qf7
18. Bxg7 Qxg7 19. Qxg7+ Kxg7 20. Rxe7+ Rf7 21. Rae1 {White’s advantage in
Pawns and Pawn-position appears decisive. Black, it will be seen, plays the
end-game most scientifically.} Rb8 22. b3 a5 23. a3 Rbf8 24. h3 Kf6 25. Rxf7+
Rxf7 26. Re3 Nb7 27. Rf3+ Ke7 28. Rxf7+ {It is very questionable policy to
deprive himself of the aid of his only' long-distance piece until his game is
further advanced.} Kxf7 29. Nf3 Kf6 30. Ne2 c5 31. Nf4 c6 32. g4 Nd6 33. Nh5+ {
Not much use in this. To keep the N in the centre of the board for the present,
offers more chances.} Ke7 34. Kf1 Nb5 35. a4 Na3 36. Ne1 c4 37. Ke2 c5 38. Kd2
d4 ({Stockfish 16:} 38... cxb3 39. cxb3 c4 $11) 39. Kc1 $16 c3 40. Nf4 Kd6 41.
Nf3 Bc6 42. Ng5 c4 {Very good. His Bishop is well placed for any emergency.}
43. Ne2 (43. Nxh7 Be4 44. Nf6 Bxc2 {, etc.}) ({Stockfish 16:} 43. Nf7+ {
or 43 h4=}) 43... Nxc2 {Fine play again.} 44. bxc4 ({If} 44. Kxc2 d3+ 45. Kxc3
dxe2 46. Kd2 cxb3 {, etc.}) 44... Bxa4 45. Ne4+ Kc6 46. f4 Bb3 $19 47. f5 a4
48. f6 Bxc4 ({Stockfish 16:} 48... a3 $1 49. N2xc3 dxc3 50. Nxc3 Kd7 $19) 49.
N2xc3 dxc3 50. Kxc2 a3 51. Kxc3 a2 52. Kb2 h6 53. g5 h5 ({Stockfish 16:} 53...
hxg5 $11) 54. g6 Kd7 55. Ng3 ({Stockfish 16:} 55. h4 Ke6 56. f7 Ke7 57. Ng5 $18
) 55... Bd3 $1 56. g7 Bc4 57. Nxh5 Ke8 58. Ng3 Kf7 59. Ne4 Bd5 60. Nc3 Be6 61.
Ne4 {This is *not* an instance of the superiority of N over Bishop in an
end-game, but a very useful study notwithstanding. Source: BCM, July 1890, p308
} 1/2-1/2

