1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 dxe4 5. Nxe4 Nbd7 6. Nf3 Be7 7. Nxf6+ Nxf6
8. Bd3 Qd6 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O Rd8 11. Rad1 Bd7 12. Rfe1 Rac8 13. Ne5 Be8 14. c3
c5 15. Bf4 Nd5 16. Bg3 cxd4 17. Nxf7 {Very well played; displaying at once
ingenuity as well as judgment. (In the text this note links back to Black's
previous move but must be a typesetting error - JS)} dxc3 18. bxc3 Qc6 19. Nxd8
Rxd8 20. Be5 Bh5 21. Rb1 Nb6 22. Qe3 Bf7 23. Qg3 Bf8 24. Bxh7+ Kxh7 25. Qh4+
Kg8 26. Qxd8 Nc4 27. Bd4 a6 28. Qb8 b5 29. a4 bxa4 30. Qd8 {Making a fit place
for his Q R.} Qd6 31. Rb8 {This game exhibits considerable smartness and
strength on White's part, but the acknowledged genius of Mr. Wainwright seems
to wane-right away as the contest progresses. (Evidently, terrible chess puns
are not confined to the 21st century - JS)} a3 32. Qxd6 Nxd6 33. Bc5 Ne8 34.
Bxf8 {Source: Illustrated Sporting and Dramatic News - Saturday 19 July 1890 
(which gives the date - it is not entirely clear whether this was the
scheduled day or whether it was played a day early)} 1-0

