[SourceVersionDate "2023.09.30"]
[SourceQuality "1"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Bxc6 dxc6 5. Nxe5 Qd4 6. Nf3 Qxe4+ 7. Qe2 Nf6
8. h3 Bf5 9. d3 Qxe2+ 10. Kxe2 Bd6 11. Nc3 O-O 12. Re1 Rfe8+ 13. Kf1 Rxe1+ 14.
Kxe1 Re8+ 15. Ne2 Nd5 16. Kf1 Nb4 17. Ned4 Nxc2 18. Nxc2 (18. Nxf5 Nxa1 19. Ne3
{best, threatening to win the Kt or regain the exchange.}) 18... Bxd3+ 19. Kg1
Bxc2 20. Bd2 Bf5 {(The score gives B to QB4 but this doesn't make sense - JS)}
21. Rc1 Rd8 22. Bc3 Be4 23. Nd2 Bd5 (23... Bf4 {looks tempting, but only leads
to exchange.}) 24. b3 f6 25. a3 {An oversight.} Bxa3 26. Ra1 Bc5 27. b4 Bb6 28.
Nf1 Re8 29. Ng3 c5 30. bxc5 Bxc5 31. Ra5 b6 32. Rxa6 {This P is hardly an
advantage, as Black now forces off the R's and must win.} Ra8 33. Rxa8+ Bxa8
34. Kh1 {Having inadvertently moved 34 P to B3 dis ch. [i.e. White had tried
to play the illegal move 34 f3, which Miss Rudge (jokingly?) refers to as a
'discovered check'.]} Bxf2 35. Ne4 {Overlooking Bxe4.} Bxe4 {Source: Northern
Whig - Thursday 06 November 1890. "Game played at the Cambridge meeting
between Mrs. Ludovici, St Albans, and Miss M. Rudge, Bristol." Sarah Ann(e)
Ludovici, née Rogers, born 3rd qtr of 1837 in Stafford, died 27 July 1904,
Wiesbaden, Germany. She married Heinrich Ludovici in 1865 in England and seems
to have lived in Germany thereafter.} 0-1

